OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026", "title": "Turn-Based Stochastic Mean-Payoff Games in Deterministic Quasipolynomial Time", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/stochastic-mean-payoff-games.pdf", "family": "104", "family_title": "Quasipolynomial algorithms for mean-payoff, stochastic and parity games", "abstract_html": "We give a uniform deterministic quasipolynomial-time algorithm for finite turn-based stochastic mean-payoff games with signed integer rewards and rational chance-transition probabilities encoded in binary. It computes exactly the vertices of nonnegative value, including value zero, for the expectation of the pathwise liminf mean payoff. The algorithm uses exact rational arithmetic and \\(2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}\\) bit operations, where <i>L</i> is the complete binary input length.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">An instance is a finite directed multigraph \\(G=(V,E)\\), with \\(V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), \\(n\\ge 1\\), and at least one outgoing edge at every vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">The vertex set is partitioned as\n  \\[\n  V=V_{\\max}\\sqcup V_{\\min}\\sqcup V_{\\mathrm{ch}}.\n  \\]\n  Max chooses the outgoing edge at vertices in \\(V_{\\max}\\), Min chooses it at vertices in \\(V_{\\min}\\), and at \\(i\\in V_{\\mathrm{ch}}\\) the outgoing edge is drawn from a prescribed rational probability distribution \\(p_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every edge \\(e\\) has an integer reward \\(w(e)\\). Rewards and probability numerators and positive denominators are encoded in binary. The graph and all edge data are listed explicitly. Loops, parallel edges, zero probabilities, and unreduced fractions are permitted.</p><p class=\"li\">Both players observe the complete finite history, including traversed edges, and may use arbitrary behavioral strategies with private randomness. Chance draws have the prescribed conditional law at the current vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">For a play \\(\\pi=(X_0,e_0,X_1,e_1,\\ldots)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi)\n  =\\liminf_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}w(e_t),\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{val}(i)\n  =\\sup_\\sigma\\inf_\\tau\n    \\mathbb E_{i,\\sigma,\\tau}[\\operatorname{MP}_w],\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\sigma,\\tau\\) range over Max's and Min's behavioral strategies. The expectation is taken after the pathwise lower limit.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the complete binary input length, and put\n  \\[\n  U=\\{i\\in V:\\operatorname{val}(i)\\ge 0\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a uniform deterministic Turing machine and an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that, on every valid input described above, the machine outputs exactly \\(U\\) in at most\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}\n\\]\nbit operations. The bound includes input reading, exact rational arithmetic, and output. Vertices of value zero are included.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 supplies an explicit discount\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac1{32H^2}\n\\]\nof polynomial binary length, a nonzero-value gap of at least \\(1/H\\), and the approximation\n\\[\n\\|v(\\lambda)-\\operatorname{val}\\|_\\infty\\le\\frac1{16H}\n\\]\nfor the expected pathwise lower-average payoff.</p><p>Proposition 2 supplies the simultaneous labeling procedure for the monotone scaled discounted map. Proposition 3 uses those labels to shrink an integer box while retaining two nearby integer comparison vectors around its fixed point. Its final integer tests output exactly the vertices of nonnegative value, including value zero.</p><p>Proposition 4 implements the complete procedure uniformly with exact arithmetic in\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. Thus there is one absolute constant \\(C\\) giving the bound in the theorem, including input reading and output.</p><p>As the consequence stated in Section 1.2 of the paper, this also gives a deterministic quasipolynomial algorithm for deciding whether the reachability value of a simple stochastic game is at least \\(1/2\\). Make the target absorbing, with reward \\(+1\\) on its self-loop, and give every other edge reward \\(-1\\); any other terminal vertex receives a self-loop of reward \\(-1\\). For every strategy pair, the pathwise mean payoff is\n\\[\n2\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{target is reached}\\}}-1.\n\\]\nIts expectation is twice the reachability probability minus one, so taking the strategy extrema proves the reduction. The transformation increases the input length by at most a constant factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1, quantitative discount reduction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a game and the expected-pathwise-liminf payoff convention of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(n=|V|\\), and define\n  \\[\n  W=\\max\\left(1,\\max_{e\\in E}|w(e)|\\right),\n  \\qquad\n  Q=\\prod_{\\substack{e\\text{ outgoing from}\\\\i\\in V_{\\mathrm{ch}}}}\n       \\operatorname{den}(p_i(e)),\n  \\]\n  where each denominator is the positive denominator supplied in the input; an empty product is one.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  A_0=n![Q(3+W)]^n,\\qquad\n  H=(W+4)A_0,\\qquad\n  \\lambda_0=\\frac1{32H^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;u&lt;1\\), define \\(f_u:\\mathbb R^V\\to\\mathbb R^V\\) by taking, at vertex \\(i\\), the maximum, minimum, or prescribed chance average of\n  \\[\n  uw(e)+(1-u)t_j,\\qquad e:i\\to j,\n  \\]\n  according as \\(i\\in V_{\\max},V_{\\min},V_{\\mathrm{ch}}\\). Let \\(v(u)\\) denote its unique fixed point.</p><p class=\"li\">A pure positional strategy chooses one outgoing edge at each vertex of its player, independently of the preceding history.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are vectors \\(G,\\psi\\in\\mathbb R^V\\) and a pure positional strategy pair \\((\\sigma^*,\\tau^*)\\) whose selected edges attain the extrema defining \\(f_u(v(u))\\) for every\n\\[\n0&lt;u\\le\\frac1{2H}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nv_i(u)=G_i+u\\psi_i+O(u^2)\\qquad(u\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nFor every start vertex \\(i\\) and every behavioral opposing strategy,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{i,\\sigma^*,\\tau}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\ge G_i,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{i,\\sigma,\\tau^*}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\le G_i.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(G_i=\\operatorname{val}(i)\\). Each \\(G_i\\) is rational with a representation whose denominator has absolute value at most \\(A_0\\), and\n\\[\n|G_i|\\le W,\\qquad\nG_i\\ne0\\Longrightarrow |G_i|\\ge\\frac1H,\n\\qquad\n|v_i(\\lambda_0)-G_i|\\le\\frac1{16H}.\n\\]\nThe integer \\(H\\), and hence \\(\\lambda_0\\), has polynomial binary length:\n\\[\n\\log H\n=O\\!\\left(n\\bigl(\\log(n+1)+\\log Q+\\log(W+3)\\bigr)\\right)\n=O((L+2)^2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 supplies a single pure positional pair \\((\\sigma^*,\\tau^*)\\) valid for every \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\). Lemma 1.2 supplies its gain \\(G\\), bias \\(\\psi\\), rational denominator bound, nonzero-value gap, and approximation estimate. It remains to identify \\(G_i\\) with the stated mean-payoff value.</p><p>For a selected player edge \\(e:i\\to j\\), substitution of the expansion into the discounted equation gives\n\\[\nG_i=G_j,\\qquad\n\\psi_i=w(e)+\\psi_j-G_j.\n\\]\nAt a chance vertex,\n\\[\nG_i=\\sum_{e:i\\to j}p_i(e)G_j,\n\\qquad\n\\psi_i=\\sum_{e:i\\to j}p_i(e)\\bigl(w(e)+\\psi_j-G_j\\bigr).\n\\]\nAt a Min vertex, the inequality\n\\[\nv_i(u)\\le uw(e)+(1-u)v_j(u)\n\\]\nfor an arbitrary outgoing edge first gives \\(G_i\\le G_j\\). If \\(G_i=G_j\\), subtracting this common constant, dividing by \\(u\\), and letting \\(u\\downarrow0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\psi_i\\le w(e)+\\psi_j-G_j.\n\\]\nAt a Max vertex, the gain inequality and, when the gains agree, the bias inequality both reverse.</p><p>Fix a start vertex \\(i\\), fix Max to \\(\\sigma^*\\), and let Min use an arbitrary behavioral strategy. Write \\(X_t\\) for the vertex before \\(e_t\\) is traversed, and let \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) be the sigma-field generated by the complete observed history through \\(X_t\\). At a chance vertex, the conditional law of \\(e_t\\) is the prescribed distribution. At a player vertex it is the distribution induced by that player's strategy, including any private randomness.</p><p>The process \\(G_{X_t}\\) has nonnegative conditional drift. A selected Max edge preserves \\(G\\); every Min edge weakly increases \\(G\\); and the chance gain identity gives zero drift at a chance vertex. Lemma 1.3 gives an eventual value \\(G_\\infty\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathbb E G_\\infty\\ge G_i.\n\\]</p><p>Define\n\\[\nR_t=w(e_t)+\\psi_{X_{t+1}}-\\psi_{X_t}-G_{X_t},\n\\qquad\nD_t=\\mathbf1_{\\{X_t\\in V_{\\mathrm{ch}}\\}}R_t.\n\\]\nThese variables are uniformly bounded because the arena is finite. At a chance vertex, the gain and bias identities imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[R_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]\n=\n\\sum_{e:X_t\\to j}p_{X_t}(e)\\bigl(w(e)+\\psi_j\\bigr)\n-\\psi_{X_t}-G_{X_t}\n=0.\n\\]\nOutside chance vertices \\(D_t=0\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[D_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=0,\n\\]\nand Lemma 1.4 gives \\(T^{-1}\\sum_{t&lt;T}D_t\\to0\\) almost surely.</p><p>After \\(G_{X_t}\\) has become constant, every traversed nonchance edge preserves \\(G\\). Its residual is zero at Max vertices and nonnegative at Min vertices. The nonchance residuals are therefore nonnegative except for a finite initial segment. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\liminf_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}R_t\\ge0\n\\quad\\text{almost surely}.\n\\]\nTelescoping the bias terms gives\n\\[\n\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}w(e_t)\n=\n\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}G_{X_t}\n+\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}R_t\n+\\frac{\\psi_{X_0}-\\psi_{X_T}}T.\n\\]\nThe first average tends to \\(G_\\infty\\), and the last term tends to zero. Hence \\(\\operatorname{MP}_w\\ge G_\\infty\\) almost surely. Taking expectations proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{i,\\sigma^*,\\tau}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\ge G_i.\n\\]</p><p>For the other inequality, fix Min to \\(\\tau^*\\) and let Max be arbitrary. Now \\(-G_{X_t}\\) has nonnegative conditional drift. Lemma 1.3 yields an eventual gain \\(G_\\infty\\) with \\(\\mathbb E G_\\infty\\le G_i\\). The chance residuals still have average zero. At Min vertices the selected edges have residual zero; at Max vertices an edge preserving \\(G\\) has nonpositive residual. Thus the nonchance residuals are eventually nonpositive, and the same telescoping identity gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}w(e_t)\\le G_\\infty\n\\quad\\text{almost surely}.\n\\]\nThis also bounds the pathwise lower average. Taking expectations proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{i,\\sigma,\\tau^*}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\le G_i.\n\\]</p><p>Max can therefore guarantee \\(G_i\\), and Min can guarantee that the payoff is at most \\(G_i\\), so \\(\\operatorname{val}(i)=G_i\\). The payoffs and gain limits in these comparisons are bounded. The argument permits every opposing history-dependent distribution and establishes the expected-liminf convention directly.</p><p>Finally, the explicit input gives \\(n=O(L)\\) and \\(\\log Q,\\log W=O(L)\\), because denominator and reward bits are included in \\(L\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\log H\n=O\\!\\left(n\\bigl(\\log(n+1)+\\log Q+\\log(W+3)\\bigr)\\right)\n=O((L+2)^2),\n\\]\nso \\(\\lambda_0\\) has polynomial bit length.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Section 2.1, a positional pair valid for all small discounts", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the game, \\(W,Q,A_0,H,\\lambda_0\\), and discounted maps of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed pure positional pair, let \\(P\\) be its row-stochastic transition matrix and \\(r_i\\) its expected one-step reward at vertex \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a polynomial \\(a\\), let \\(\\|a\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\) be the sum of the absolute values of its coefficients.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(f_u\\) is a contraction in the maximum norm with constant \\(1-u\\), has a unique fixed point, and\n\\[\n\\|v(u)\\|_\\infty\\le W.\n\\]\nFor every fixed pure positional pair, its discounted solution \\(x(u)\\) satisfies\n\\[\nx(u)=ur+(1-u)Px(u),\\qquad \\|x(u)\\|_\\infty\\le W,\n\\]\nand has representations\n\\[\nx_i(u)=\\frac{N_i(u)}{D(u)},\n\\qquad\nD(u)=\\det\\!\\bigl(Q(I-(1-u)P)\\bigr),\n\\]\nwith integer polynomials satisfying\n\\[\n\\|D\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\le A_0,\\qquad\n\\|N_i\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\le A_0.\n\\]\nFor every player edge \\(e:i\\to j\\), the sign of\n\\[\nx_i(u)-uw(e)-(1-u)x_j(u)\n\\]\nis constant, or the expression vanishes identically, throughout \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\).</p><p>In particular, there is a pure positional pair \\((\\sigma^*,\\tau^*)\\) whose linear discounted solution equals \\(v(u)\\) and whose selected edges attain all player extrema for every \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each expression\n\\[\nuw(e)+(1-u)t_j\n\\]\nchanges by at most \\((1-u)\\|t-t'\\|_\\infty\\) when \\(t\\) is replaced by \\(t'\\). Taking an extremum or an average preserves that bound. Thus \\(f_u\\) is a contraction. Its iterates from zero converge, because the successive differences decrease geometrically. Their limit is the unique fixed point. Since \\(f_u\\) preserves \\([-W,W]^V\\),\n\\[\n\\|v(u)\\|_\\infty\\le W.\n\\]</p><p>Temporarily fix a pure positional pair. At a chance vertex, transition probabilities of parallel edges are added in \\(P\\), whereas their possibly different rewards are averaged in \\(r\\). Its discounted equations are\n\\[\nx(u)=ur+(1-u)Px(u).\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(I-(1-u)P\\) is invertible for \\(0&lt;u&lt;1\\): a vector in its kernel would satisfy\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le(1-u)\\|x\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nThe corresponding affine contraction preserves \\([-W,W]^V\\), so its solution satisfies \\(\\|x(u)\\|_\\infty\\le W\\).</p><p>The coefficient norm is subadditive and submultiplicative. Every entry of \\(QP\\) and \\(Qr\\) is an integer. Cramer's rule therefore gives integer polynomials \\(N_i,D\\) with\n\\[\nx_i(u)=\\frac{N_i(u)}{D(u)},\n\\qquad\nD(u)=\\det\\!\\bigl(Q(I-(1-u)P)\\bigr).\n\\]\nEach entry of the matrix defining \\(D\\) has coefficient norm at most \\(3Q\\). The replacing column defining \\(N_i\\) is \\(Qur\\), whose entries have coefficient norm at most \\(QW\\). Expanding each determinant over its \\(n!\\) permutations gives\n\\[\n\\|D\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\le A_0,\\qquad\n\\|N_i\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\le A_0.\n\\]</p><p>Whether this pair's chosen edge is optimal at a player vertex \\(i\\) is determined by comparisons with every outgoing edge \\(e:i\\to j\\). Each comparison is the sign of\n\\[\nx_i(u)-uw(e)-(1-u)x_j(u)\n=\n\\frac{N_i(u)-uw(e)D(u)-(1-u)N_j(u)}{D(u)}.\n\\]\nThe numerator has coefficient norm at most \\((3+W)A_0\\le H\\).</p><p>Let \\(a\\) be any nonzero integer polynomial with \\(\\|a\\|_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\le H\\). Factoring its lowest power of \\(u\\) gives\n\\[\na(u)=u^\\ell(c+uq(u)),\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is a nonzero integer. For \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\),\n\\[\n|uq(u)|\\le Hu\\le\\frac12&lt;|c|.\n\\]\nThus the sign of \\(a(u)\\) throughout this interval is the sign of its lowest nonzero coefficient. This applies to both the numerator and denominator of every edge comparison; the denominator is nonzero there by invertibility. Every edge comparison therefore has a constant sign or vanishes identically on the interval.</p><p>Now choose attaining edges in the maximum and minimum defining \\(f_{\\lambda_0}(v(\\lambda_0))\\). They give a pure positional pair \\((\\sigma^*,\\tau^*)\\), and its linear solution at \\(\\lambda_0\\) equals \\(v(\\lambda_0)\\). Since \\(\\lambda_0\\le1/(2H)\\), all edge inequalities holding at \\(\\lambda_0\\) continue to hold for every \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\). For each such \\(u\\), the pair's linear solution is consequently a fixed point of \\(f_u\\), hence equals \\(v(u)\\). The pair is needed for the proof; the algorithm need not compute it.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Section 2.2, the rational gain and its approximation error", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a pure positional pair whose discounted solution equals \\(v(u)\\) for every \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_i,D\\) be its integer Cramer polynomials, with coefficient norms at most \\(A_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are vectors \\(G,\\psi\\in\\mathbb R^V\\) such that\n\\[\nv_i(u)=G_i+u\\psi_i+O(u^2)\\qquad(u\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nEach \\(G_i\\) has a rational representation with denominator of absolute value at most \\(A_0\\), and\n\\[\n|G_i|\\le W,\\qquad\nG_i\\ne0\\Longrightarrow |G_i|\\ge\\frac1{A_0}\\ge\\frac1H.\n\\]\nFor every \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\),\n\\[\n|v_i(u)-G_i|\\le2Hu.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n|v_i(\\lambda_0)-G_i|\\le\\frac1{16H}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\ell\\) be the lowest nonzero degree of \\(D\\). Boundedness of \\(N_i(u)/D(u)\\) as \\(u\\downarrow0\\) implies that \\(u^\\ell\\) also divides each \\(N_i\\). Define\n\\[\n\\widehat D=D/u^\\ell,\\qquad\n\\widehat N_i=N_i/u^\\ell.\n\\]\nTheir coefficient norms are at most \\(A_0\\), and \\(\\widehat D(0)\\) is a nonzero integer. The quotient is analytic at zero, so it has the stated expansion, with\n\\[\nG_i=\\frac{\\widehat N_i(0)}{\\widehat D(0)}.\n\\]\nThe bound \\(\\|v(u)\\|_\\infty\\le W\\) gives \\(|G_i|\\le W\\). The displayed denominator has magnitude at most \\(A_0\\), and its numerator is an integer. Thus \\(G_i\\ne0\\) implies\n\\[\n|G_i|\\ge\\frac1{A_0}\\ge\\frac1H.\n\\]</p><p>The polynomial \\(\\widehat N_i-G_i\\widehat D\\) has constant coefficient zero and coefficient norm at most \\((1+W)A_0\\). For \\(0&lt;u\\le1/(2H)\\), the tail of \\(\\widehat D\\) has magnitude at most \\(A_0u\\le1/2\\), so\n\\[\n|\\widehat D(u)|\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\n|v_i(u)-G_i|\n\\le2(1+W)A_0u\n\\le2Hu.\n\\]\nAt \\(u=\\lambda_0=1/(32H^2)\\), this gives the claimed error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.2, finite-valued submartingales", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((Z_t)_{t\\ge0}\\) be adapted to a filtration \\((\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The variables \\(Z_t\\) take values in a fixed finite subset of \\(\\mathbb R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(t\\),\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E[Z_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]\\ge Z_t.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The process \\(Z_t\\) is eventually constant almost surely. Its eventual value \\(Z_\\infty\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E Z_\\infty\\ge\\mathbb E Z_0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Add a constant to make \\(Y_t=Z_t+c\\) nonnegative. Conditional expectation and the assumed drift give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[Y_{t+1}^2-Y_t^2]\n&amp;=\\mathbb E[(Y_{t+1}-Y_t)^2]\n  +2\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n      Y_t\\mathbb E[Y_{t+1}-Y_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]\n    \\right]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\mathbb E[(Y_{t+1}-Y_t)^2].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSumming and using boundedness shows that\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{t\\ge0}(Y_{t+1}-Y_t)^2&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThe sum is therefore finite almost surely. If there is more than one possible value, let \\(\\Delta&gt;0\\) be the minimum distance between distinct values. Every change contributes at least \\(\\Delta^2\\) to the sum, so only finitely many changes occur. The singleton case is immediate. Finally, \\(\\mathbb E Z_t\\ge\\mathbb E Z_0\\) for all \\(t\\), and bounded convergence gives the claimed expectation inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.3, averages of bounded martingale differences", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0}\\) be a filtration.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(D_t\\) is \\(\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\)-measurable,\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb E[D_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=0,\n  \\qquad\n  |D_t|\\le c\n  \\]\n  for a fixed finite constant \\(c\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Almost surely,\n\\[\n\\frac1T\\sum_{t&lt;T}D_t\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(s&lt;t\\), conditioning on \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) gives \\(\\mathbb E[D_sD_t]=0\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left[\\left(\\sum_{t&lt;T}D_t\\right)^2\\right]\\le Tc^2.\n\\]\nChebyshev's inequality at \\(T=j^2\\) gives, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left(\n \\left|\\frac1{j^2}\\sum_{t&lt;j^2}D_t\\right|&gt;\\varepsilon\n\\right)\n\\le\\frac{c^2}{\\varepsilon^2j^2}.\n\\]\nThe sum over \\(j\\) is finite. The probability that one of these events occurs after index \\(m\\) is at most the tail of this sum, which tends to zero. Applying this argument to \\(\\varepsilon=1,1/2,1/3,\\ldots\\) proves convergence along squares on a single event of probability one. For \\(j^2\\le T&lt;(j+1)^2\\), the additional partial sum has magnitude at most \\(c(2j+1)\\). Dividing by \\(T\\ge j^2\\) extends convergence to every \\(T\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Theorem 3.2, recursive simultaneous labeling. The witness definitions are original Definition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge1\\), and let \\(F:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  t\\le t'\\Longrightarrow F(t)\\le F(t'),\n  \\qquad\n  F(t+c\\mathbf1)=F(t)+\\gamma c\\mathbf1,\n  \\]\n  for all \\(t,t'\\in\\mathbb R^n\\), \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\), where \\(0&lt;\\gamma&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All vector inequalities, extrema, and integer rounding are coordinatewise.</p><p class=\"li\">An input consists of \\(A\\in\\mathbb Z^n\\), positive rational mass vectors \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^n\\), and a width\n  \\[\n  D=h2^d,\\qquad\n  128\\le h\\le255,\\qquad h,d\\in\\mathbb Z,\\qquad d\\ge0.\n  \\]\n  This representation of \\(D\\) is unique. Write\n  \\[\n  u=A+D\\mathbf1,\\qquad P=D/32,\n  \\qquad\n  a(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}a_i,\\qquad b(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}b_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An integer vector \\(y\\in[A,u]\\) is a subsolution in the box if\n  \\[\n  F_i(y)\\ge y_i\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in S_y,\n  \\qquad\n  S_y=\\{i:y_i&gt;A_i\\}.\n  \\]\n  It is a plus witness if \\(a(S_y)\\le1\\); it claims each index \\(i\\) with \\(y_i\\ge u_i-P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An integer vector \\(z\\in[A,u]\\) is a supersolution in the box if\n  \\[\n  F_i(z)\\le z_i\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in S_z,\n  \\qquad\n  S_z=\\{i:z_i&lt;u_i\\}.\n  \\]\n  It is a minus witness if \\(b(S_z)\\le1\\); it claims each index \\(i\\) with \\(z_i\\le A_i+P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The sets \\(S_y,S_z\\) are the respective supports. A witness is relevant if it has at least one claim. Every claim belongs to its witness's support, since \\(0&lt;P&lt;D\\).</p><p>Define the procedure \\(\\mathcal L(A,D,a,b)\\) as follows. It uses \\(F\\) only through exact evaluations of \\(\\lfloor F(t)\\rfloor\\) at integer vectors \\(t\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Mass base case.** If \\(a_ib_i&gt;1\\) for every \\(i\\), return \\(+\\) at \\(i\\) when \\(b_i&gt;1\\), and return \\(-\\) otherwise.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Width base case.** If \\(d&lt;12\\), start at \\(t^0=u\\) and iterate\n   \\[\n   t^{m+1}=T(t^m),\n   \\qquad\n   T(t)=\\min\\{u,\\max\\{A,\\lfloor F(t)\\rfloor\\}\\}\n   \\]\n   until an unchanged vector \\(t^*\\) is reached. Return \\(+\\) at \\(i\\) when\n   \\[\n   t_i^*&gt;A_i+D/2,\n   \\]\n   and return \\(-\\) otherwise.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If neither base case applies, set\n   \\[\n   c=A+\\frac D2\\mathbf1,\\qquad\n   M=\\frac D8,\\qquad\n   q=\\frac D{128},\\qquad\n   p=\\frac q{32}=\\frac D{4096}.\n   \\]\n   A centered call of even width \\(r\\) at an integer pivot \\(k\\), with masses \\(a',b'\\), means\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L\\!\\left(k-\\frac r2\\mathbf1,r,a',b'\\right).\n   \\]</p><p>   **Downward pass.** Initialize \\(k=c\\). Make a centered width-\\(q\\) call at \\(k\\) with masses \\((a,\\frac43b)\\), and update all coordinates simultaneously:\n   \\[\n   k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\max\\{c_i-M,k_i-p\\},&amp;\\text{if the child labels }i\\text{ by }-,\\\\\n   k_i,&amp;\\text{if the child labels }i\\text{ by }+.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Replace \\(k\\) by \\(k^{\\mathrm{new}}\\) and repeat until an update leaves the pivot unchanged. Denote the last pivot by \\(k^\\downarrow\\), and set\n   \\[\n   I_-=\\{i:k_i^\\downarrow&lt;c_i-P\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. **Upward pass.** Start at \\(k=k^\\downarrow\\). Make a centered width-\\(q\\) call at \\(k\\) with masses \\((\\frac43a,b)\\), and update simultaneously:\n   \\[\n   k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\min\\{c_i+M,k_i+p\\},&amp;\\text{if the child labels }i\\text{ by }+,\\\\\n   k_i,&amp;\\text{if the child labels }i\\text{ by }-.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Repeat until an unchanged update. Denote the last pivot by \\(k^f\\), and set\n   \\[\n   I_+=\\{i:k_i^f&gt;c_i+P\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. **Provisional labels and new masses.** Make a centered width-\\(D/2\\) call at \\(k^f\\) with masses \\((a,b)\\). For its output \\(\\ell^0\\), define\n   \\[\n   (\\bar a_i,\\bar b_i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (\\frac45a_i,\\frac85b_i),&amp;\\ell_i^0=+,\\\\\n   (\\frac85a_i,\\frac45b_i),&amp;\\ell_i^0=-.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. **Continuation and overrides.** Call \\(\\mathcal L(A,D,\\bar a,\\bar b)\\). In its output replace the signs on \\(I_-\\) by \\(-\\), and those on \\(I_+\\setminus I_-\\) by \\(+\\). Return this label vector.</p><p>Each pass includes the final child call whose update changes nothing. All coordinates in an update use the same old pivot and the same child output.</p><p>For a relevant parent plus witness \\(y\\) and a pivot \\(k\\in[c-M\\mathbf1,c+M\\mathbf1]\\), define\n\\[\ns_y(k)=\\max_i(y_i-k_i),\n\\qquad\ng_i^y(k)=s_y(k)-(y_i-k_i).\n\\]\nFor a relevant parent minus witness \\(z\\), define\n\\[\ns_z(k)=\\max_i(k_i-z_i),\n\\qquad\ng_i^z(k)=s_z(k)-(k_i-z_i).\n\\]\nThe gaps are nonnegative integers, and at least one gap is zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The procedure terminates deterministically and returns \\(\\ell\\in\\{+,-\\}^n\\) satisfying, simultaneously for all witnesses,\n\\[\ny_i\\ge A_i+D-P\\Longrightarrow\\ell_i=+\n\\quad\\text{for every plus witness }y,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nz_i\\le A_i+P\\Longrightarrow\\ell_i=-\n\\quad\\text{for every minus witness }z.\n\\]\nThe algorithm is not given any witness and does not search for one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Termination follows from Lemma 2.3. Prove the labeling guarantee by induction on the same rank \\(d+B(\\mu(a,b))\\).</p><p>In the mass base case, an index labeled \\(+\\) has \\(b_i&gt;1\\), so no minus witness can contain that index in its support or claim it. At an index labeled \\(-\\), one has \\(b_i\\le1\\) and \\(a_ib_i&gt;1\\), hence \\(a_i&gt;1\\); no plus witness can claim that index. This proves both guarantees.</p><p>In the width base case, Lemma 2.1 gives\n\\[\ny\\le t^*\\le z\n\\]\nfor all witnesses. A plus claim forces\n\\[\nt_i^*\\ge u_i-P&gt;A_i+D/2;\n\\]\na minus claim forces\n\\[\nt_i^*\\le A_i+P&lt;A_i+D/2.\n\\]\nThus the returned signs are correct.</p><p>For a nonbase call, assume correctness of all its children, which have smaller rank by Lemma 2.3. Lemmas 2.5 and 2.6 therefore apply. Let\n\\[\nQ_+=\\{i:\\ell_i^0=+\\},\n\\qquad\nQ_-=\\{i:\\ell_i^0=-\\}\n\\]\nbe the provisional sign sets.</p><p>Consider a plus witness \\(y\\) that has a claim outside \\(I_+\\). By Lemma 2.6,\n\\[\nG_y=\\{i:g_i^y(k^f)&lt;q\\}\n\\]\nhas \\(a\\)-mass greater than \\(3/4\\). It lies in \\(S_y\\) by Lemma 2.4. Apply that lemma with width \\(r=D/2\\) to the provisional call. Its plus masses are unchanged, so the translated vector is a plus witness. Since\n\\[\nq=D/128&lt;D/64=r/32,\n\\]\nevery index in \\(G_y\\) is a claim of this translated witness. All these indices belong to \\(Q_+\\) by the child's guarantee. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\bar a(S_y)\n=\\frac85a(S_y)-\\frac45a(S_y\\cap Q_+)\n&lt;\\frac85-\\frac45\\cdot\\frac34\n=1.\n\\]\nThe parent vector \\(y\\) is still a subsolution in the unchanged box, so it is a plus witness for the continuation.</p><p>For a minus witness \\(z\\) having a claim outside \\(I_-\\), the set\n\\[\nG_z=\\{i:g_i^z(k^f)&lt;q\\}\n\\]\nhas \\(b\\)-mass greater than \\(3/4\\) by Lemma 2.6. It lies in \\(S_z\\). The width-\\(D/2\\) minus translation is a witness for the provisional call because the minus masses are unchanged; every index in \\(G_z\\) is one of its claims, and hence belongs to \\(Q_-\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\bar b(S_z)\n=\\frac85b(S_z)-\\frac45b(S_z\\cap Q_-)\n&lt;\\frac85-\\frac45\\cdot\\frac34\n=1.\n\\]\nThe same vector \\(z\\) is a minus witness for the continuation.</p><p>It remains to check every claim after the overrides. Let \\(y\\) claim \\(i\\). Lemma 2.5 gives \\(i\\notin I_-\\). If \\(i\\in I_+\\), it receives the plus override. If \\(i\\notin I_+\\), the preceding mass bound applies to \\(y\\), the continuation labels \\(i\\) by \\(+\\), and no override changes it.</p><p>Now let \\(z\\) claim \\(i\\). Lemma 2.6 gives \\(i\\notin I_+\\). If \\(i\\in I_-\\), it receives the minus override. Otherwise the preceding minus-mass bound applies to \\(z\\), and the continuation's minus label is unchanged. In particular, \\(I_-\\cap I_+\\) contains no claim of either sign, so the stated priority between overrides causes no conflict. This completes the induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, comparison and a finite box iteration", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the map \\(F\\), integer box \\([A,u]\\), and notions of subsolution and supersolution from Proposition 2. No mass condition is imposed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every subsolution \\(y\\) and supersolution \\(z\\) in the same box satisfy \\(y\\le z\\). Starting at \\(t^0=u\\), the iteration\n\\[\nt^{m+1}=T(t^m),\n\\qquad\nT(t)=\\min\\{u,\\max\\{A,\\lfloor F(t)\\rfloor\\}\\}\n\\]\nstops at a vector \\(t^*\\) after at most \\(nD+1\\) evaluations of \\(T\\). For every such pair \\(y,z\\),\n\\[\ny\\le t^*\\le z.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If\n\\[\nm=\\max_j(y_j-z_j)&gt;0,\n\\]\nchoose \\(i\\) with \\(y_i-z_i=m\\). Since \\(y_i&gt;z_i\\ge A_i\\) and \\(z_i&lt;y_i\\le u_i\\), both defining inequalities apply at \\(i\\). Monotonicity and the common translation rule give\n\\[\nm\n\\le F_i(y)-F_i(z)\n\\le F_i(z+m\\mathbf1)-F_i(z)\n=\\gamma m,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>The map \\(T\\) preserves the finite integer box and is monotone. Moreover \\(T(u)\\le u\\), so induction gives \\(t^{m+1}\\le t^m\\). Each changing step decreases the nonnegative integer\n\\[\n\\sum_i(t_i^m-A_i)\n\\]\nby at least one. Its initial value is \\(nD\\), proving the termination bound, including the final unchanged step.</p><p>For an integer \\(y\\in[A,u]\\), the subsolution condition is equivalent to \\(y\\le T(y)\\). Indeed, when \\(y_i&gt;A_i\\), the latter inequality is equivalent to \\(y_i\\le\\lfloor F_i(y)\\rfloor\\), and at the other coordinates it is automatic. If \\(y\\) is a subsolution, induction gives \\(y\\le t^m\\): from \\(y\\le t^m\\) one obtains\n\\[\ny\\le T(y)\\le T(t^m)=t^{m+1}.\n\\]\nThus \\(y\\le t^*\\). The fixed point \\(t^*=T(t^*)\\) is itself a subsolution, and the comparison principle gives \\(t^*\\le z\\) for every supersolution \\(z\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, valid boxes and bounded passes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the inputs and recursive procedure of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every child input is valid and its box lies inside its parent's box. If the child calls terminate, each pivot pass makes at most \\(1024n+1\\) calls. The width base case makes at most\n\\[\n522240n+1\n\\]\nevaluations of \\(\\lfloor F\\rfloor\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In a nonbase call \\(d\\ge12\\), so \\(c,M,q,p\\) and the half-widths \\(q/2,D/4\\) are integral. The centered widths have the allowed representations\n\\[\nq=h2^{d-7},\\qquad D/2=h2^{d-1}.\n\\]\nThe pivot caps keep\n\\[\nc-M\\mathbf1\\le k\\le c+M\\mathbf1,\n\\]\nand a centered box has width \\(r\\le D/2\\). Its boundaries satisfy\n\\[\nk-\\frac r2\\mathbf1\\ge A+\\frac D8\\mathbf1\\ge A,\n\\qquad\nk+\\frac r2\\mathbf1\\le A+\\frac{7D}8\\mathbf1\\le u.\n\\]\nThe continuation uses the same box. All masses remain positive rationals.</p><p>Every pivot coordinate lies on the grid of spacing \\(p\\) through \\(c_i\\), because \\(M/p=512\\) is integral. In a changing update some coordinate moves by \\(p\\); each coordinate is monotone during a pass and travels at most \\(2M\\), hence moves at most \\(1024\\) times. There are at most \\(1024n\\) changing updates, followed by one unchanged update.</p><p>Finally, \\(d&lt;12\\) implies\n\\[\nD\\le255\\cdot2^{11}=522240,\n\\]\nso the width-base bound follows from Lemma 2.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, rank and branching", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the procedure of Proposition 2. Define\n\\[\n\\mu(a,b)=\\min_i a_ib_i,\n\\qquad\n\\rho=\\frac{32}{25},\n\\qquad\nB(\\mu)=\\min\\{j\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}:\\rho^j\\mu&gt;1\\}.\n\\]\nThe integer \\(B(\\mu)\\) is used only in the proof and is not computed by the procedure.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The procedure terminates. At a nonbase call, every child has smaller rank\n\\[\nd+B(\\mu(a,b)).\n\\]\nThere is exactly one child preserving \\(B\\), the provisional call, and it decreases \\(d\\) by one. There are at most \\(2048n+3\\) other children; each decreases \\(B\\) by at least one and does not increase \\(d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At a nonbase call \\(B(\\mu)&gt;0\\), since otherwise the mass base case would apply. Whenever \\(\\mu'\\ge\\rho\\mu\\), the definition gives\n\\[\nB(\\mu')\\le B(\\mu)-1.\n\\]\nBoth pivot passes multiply every mass product by \\(4/3\\ge\\rho\\). The continuation multiplies every mass product by\n\\[\n\\frac45\\cdot\\frac85=\\rho,\n\\]\nindependently of the provisional label. These children reduce \\(B\\) and preserve or reduce \\(d\\). Only the provisional call keeps the masses; it halves the width and reduces \\(d\\) by one. This proves the rank assertion.</p><p>Induction on the nonnegative rank proves termination. The base cases are finite by their definitions and Lemma 2.2. At a nonbase call every child terminates by induction, and the bound on each pivot pass then applies. The number of children decreasing \\(B\\) is at most\n\\[\n2(1024n+1)+1=2048n+3.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.6, translation and clipping", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a nonbase input of Proposition 2, with \\(c,M,q\\) as defined there.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\in[c-M\\mathbf1,c+M\\mathbf1]\\), let \\(r\\in\\{q,D/2\\}\\), and let \\(y\\) or \\(z\\) be a relevant witness of the parent box.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the corresponding peak \\(s_y(k)\\) or \\(s_z(k)\\), and gaps \\(g_i^y(k)\\) or \\(g_i^z(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define, according to the witness's sign,\n  \\[\n  y_i'=\\max\\{k_i-r/2,\\ y_i-s_y(k)+r/2\\},\n  \\]\n  or\n  \\[\n  z_i'=\\min\\{k_i+r/2,\\ z_i+s_z(k)-r/2\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting vector is an integer subsolution or supersolution, respectively, in the centered width-\\(r\\) box. Its support is exactly\n\\[\n\\{i:g_i(k)&lt;r\\},\n\\]\nwhich is contained in the parent's support. It claims every index with\n\\[\ng_i(k)\\le r/32.\n\\]\nIt is a witness for that child whenever its support has mass at most one for the child's mass vector of the corresponding sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>A parent claim gives, in either case,\n\\[\ns(k)\\ge D/2-M-P=11D/32.\n\\]\nOutside the parent support one has \\(y_i=A_i\\) or \\(z_i=u_i\\). The corresponding deviation is at most\n\\[\n-D/2+M=-3D/8,\n\\]\nso its gap is at least \\(23D/32&gt;D/2\\). Such an index cannot belong to \\(\\{i:g_i(k)&lt;r\\}\\).</p><p>Before clipping, the two translated expressions are\n\\[\nk_i+r/2-g_i^y(k),\n\\qquad\nk_i-r/2+g_i^z(k),\n\\]\nrespectively. They lie on the correct side of the far boundary; clipping at the other boundary puts the vector in the child box. These expressions also show directly that the support is exactly the set of gaps strictly below \\(r\\), and that every gap at most \\(r/32\\) gives a claim.</p><p>For the plus case let\n\\[\nt=-s_y(k)+r/2&lt;0,\n\\]\nwhere the strict sign follows from \\(s_y(k)\\ge11D/32\\) and \\(r/2\\le D/4\\). One has \\(y'\\ge y+t\\mathbf1\\). At an index in the child support, equality holds in that coordinate, and the parent subsolution inequality applies. Therefore\n\\[\nF_i(y')\n\\ge F_i(y+t\\mathbf1)\n=F_i(y)+\\gamma t\n\\ge y_i+t\n=y_i',\n\\]\nusing \\(\\gamma t\\ge t\\).</p><p>For the minus case let\n\\[\nt=s_z(k)-r/2&gt;0.\n\\]\nNow \\(z'\\le z+t\\mathbf1\\), with coordinatewise equality on the child support, and\n\\[\nF_i(z')\n\\le F_i(z+t\\mathbf1)\n=F_i(z)+\\gamma t\n\\le z_i+t\n=z_i',\n\\]\nusing \\(\\gamma t\\le t\\). Thus the required inequalities survive both translation and clipping. The stated mass condition is exactly the remaining condition for being a child witness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.7, the downward pass", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a nonbase input and the downward pass of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">In addition to the parent assumptions, assume that all child calls satisfy the labeling guarantee of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Witnesses, supports, and claims below refer to the parent input.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every relevant plus witness \\(y\\), the peak \\(s_y(k)\\) is constant throughout the downward pass and is at most \\(D/2\\). No plus witness claims an index in \\(I_-\\).</p><p>Every minus witness \\(z\\) with a claim outside \\(I_-\\) satisfies\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^z(k^\\downarrow)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a relevant plus witness \\(y\\) and consider an update from \\(k\\). The width-\\(q\\) translation in Lemma 2.4 is a plus witness for the child, since the plus masses are unchanged and its support is contained in \\(S_y\\). Its claims include all indices with\n\\[\ng_i^y(k)\\le q/32=p.\n\\]\nThe child labels all of them by \\(+\\), so they do not move. In particular, every peak achiever continues to attain the old peak.</p><p>At any other coordinate let \\(\\delta_i=k_i-k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}\\). Then \\(0\\le\\delta_i\\le p\\), and\n\\[\ny_i-k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}\n=s_y(k)-g_i^y(k)+\\delta_i\n&lt;s_y(k).\n\\]\nThus no moving coordinate reaches the old peak, even when several coordinates move simultaneously. The peak is unchanged. At the initial pivot \\(c\\), the inequality \\(y\\le u\\) gives \\(s_y(c)\\le D/2\\). If \\(y\\) claims \\(i\\), it follows that\n\\[\nk_i^\\downarrow\n\\ge y_i-D/2\n\\ge A_i+D/2-P\n=c_i-P,\n\\]\nso \\(i\\notin I_-\\).</p><p>For the mass assertion, let \\(z\\) claim \\(j\\notin I_-\\). Then\n\\[\nk_j^\\downarrow\\ge c_j-P,\\qquad z_j\\le A_j+P,\n\\]\nwhence\n\\[\ns_z(k^\\downarrow)\\ge D/2-2P=7D/16&gt;D/2-M.\n\\]\nAt a lower-capped coordinate \\(k_i^\\downarrow=c_i-M\\), one instead has\n\\[\nk_i^\\downarrow-z_i\\le D/2-M,\n\\]\nsince \\(z_i\\ge A_i\\). Every peak achiever is therefore strictly above the lower cap.</p><p>If\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^z(k^\\downarrow)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)\\le\\frac34,\n\\]\nthe width-\\(q\\) translation of \\(z\\) would have support mass at most one under the final child's minus masses \\(\\frac43b\\). It would be a minus witness and would claim every peak achiever. The child would label such a coordinate by \\(-\\), forcing it to move down because it is above the lower cap. This contradicts the unchanged update. The mass is therefore strictly greater than \\(3/4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.8, the upward pass", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a nonbase input and both passes of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">In addition to the parent assumptions, assume that all child calls satisfy the labeling guarantee of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Witnesses, supports, and claims refer to the parent input.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every relevant minus witness \\(z\\), the peak \\(s_z(k)\\) is constant throughout the upward pass and is at most \\(D/2\\). No minus witness claims an index in \\(I_+\\).</p><p>At the final pivot,\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^z(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34\n\\quad\\text{if }z\\text{ has a claim outside }I_-,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^y(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34\n\\quad\\text{if }y\\text{ has a claim outside }I_+.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a relevant minus witness \\(z\\). The width-\\(q\\) translation is a minus witness for every child in this pass because the minus masses are unchanged. All coordinates with \\(g_i^z(k)\\le p\\) must receive \\(-\\) and stay fixed. In particular, a peak achiever stays fixed. At a coordinate with \\(g_i^z(k)&gt;p\\), write\n\\[\n\\delta_i=k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}-k_i\\in[0,p].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nk_i^{\\mathrm{new}}-z_i\n=s_z(k)-g_i^z(k)+\\delta_i\n&lt;s_z(k).\n\\]\nNo coordinate overtakes the peak, so the peak is constant. At the starting pivot \\(k^\\downarrow\\le c\\) and with \\(z\\ge A\\), it is at most \\(D/2\\). A minus claim at \\(i\\) consequently implies\n\\[\nk_i^f\\le z_i+D/2\\le A_i+P+D/2=c_i+P,\n\\]\nexcluding \\(i\\) from \\(I_+\\).</p><p>Because the peak is constant and \\(k\\) only increases during this pass, every gap \\(g_i^z(k)\\) is nonincreasing. Thus the set of gaps below \\(q\\) can only grow, and the minus-mass inequality of Lemma 2.5 implies\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^z(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34\n\\]\nwhenever \\(z\\) has a claim outside \\(I_-\\).</p><p>Now let a plus witness \\(y\\) claim \\(j\\notin I_+\\). At the final pivot,\n\\[\nk_j^f\\le c_j+P,\\qquad y_j\\ge A_j+D-P,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\ns_y(k^f)\\ge D/2-2P=7D/16&gt;D/2-M.\n\\]\nAn upper-capped coordinate \\(k_i^f=c_i+M\\) has\n\\[\ny_i-k_i^f\\le D/2-M,\n\\]\nsince \\(y_i\\le u_i\\). Every peak achiever is strictly below the upper cap. If\n\\[\na\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^y(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)\\le\\frac34,\n\\]\nits width-\\(q\\) translation would be a plus witness for the last child, whose plus masses are \\(\\frac43a\\). Every peak achiever would have label \\(+\\) and would move up, contradicting the final unchanged update. This proves the remaining inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Sections 4.1–4.2, from labels to an exact decision", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a game satisfying Theorem 1, with\n  \\[\n  W=\\max\\left(1,\\max_e|w(e)|\\right),\n  \\quad\n  Q=\\prod_{\\substack{e\\text{ outgoing from}\\\\i\\in V_{\\mathrm{ch}}}}\n       \\operatorname{den}(p_i(e)),\n  \\quad\n  A_0=n![Q(3+W)]^n,\n  \\quad\n  H=(W+4)A_0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the discounted maps and fixed points of Proposition 1, and set\n  \\[\n  N=32H^2,\\qquad\n  \\lambda=1/N=\\lambda_0,\\qquad\n  \\gamma=1-1/N,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  K=256(N+1),\\qquad\n  S=16HK,\\qquad\n  J=SW+N+1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F(t)=Sf_\\lambda(t/S),\n  \\qquad\n  x=Sv(\\lambda).\n  \\]\n  Then \\(F(x)=x\\), \\(F\\) is monotone, and\n  \\[\n  F(t+c\\mathbf1)=F(t)+\\gamma c\\mathbf1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The following integer vectors are used only in the proof and are not computed:\n  \\[\n  Y=\\lfloor x\\rfloor-N\\mathbf1,\n  \\qquad\n  Z=\\lceil x\\rceil+N\\mathbf1.\n  \\]\n  Floors of negative numbers are taken toward minus infinity.</p><p>Define the outer box procedure as follows.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Set\n   \\[\n   A=-J\\mathbf1,\\qquad\n   D=D_0=128\\cdot2^{d_0},\n   \\]\n   where \\(d_0\\) is the least nonnegative integer with \\(D_0\\ge2J\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. While \\(D&gt;K\\), write \\(D=h2^d\\) in its permitted representation, call\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L(A,D,a,b),\n   \\qquad a_i=b_i=1/n,\n   \\]\n   using the map \\(F\\), and let \\(\\ell\\) be its labels.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Let the next smaller permitted width be\n   \\[\n   D'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (h-1)2^d,&amp;h&gt;128,\\\\\n   255\\cdot2^{d-1},&amp;h=128.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Put \\(\\delta=D-D'\\), and define\n   \\[\n   A_i'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   A_i+\\delta,&amp;\\ell_i=+,\\\\\n   A_i,&amp;\\ell_i=-.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Replace \\((A,D)\\) by \\((A',D')\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. At termination, output exactly the indices satisfying\n   \\[\n   A_i\\ge-8K.\n   \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every box of the procedure contains \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\). The procedure makes at most \\(128(d_0+1)\\) calls to \\(\\mathcal L\\), ends with \\(D\\le K\\), and its final lower endpoint satisfies\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{A_i}{S}-v_i(\\lambda)\\right|\\le\\frac1{16H},\n\\qquad\n\\left|\\frac{A_i}{S}-\\operatorname{val}(i)\\right|\\le\\frac1{8H}.\n\\]\nIts output is exactly\n\\[\n\\{i:\\operatorname{val}(i)\\ge0\\},\n\\]\nincluding vertices of value zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the scaled discounted map,\n\\[\nF(x)=Sf_\\lambda(v(\\lambda))=Sv(\\lambda)=x.\n\\]\nIts monotonicity and translation identity follow from the definition of \\(f_\\lambda\\). Thus Proposition 2 applies.</p><p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2 show that the procedure terminates with a box containing \\(Y,Z\\), with \\(Y\\le x\\le Z\\), and with \\(D\\le K\\). Hence\n\\[\nA_i\\le x_i\\le A_i+D,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{A_i}{S}-v_i(\\lambda)\\right|\n\\le\\frac KS\n=\\frac1{16H}.\n\\]\nProposition 1 now gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{A_i}{S}-\\operatorname{val}(i)\\right|\n\\le\\frac1{8H}.\n\\]</p><p>The final test can equivalently be written\n\\[\n\\frac{A_i}{S}\\ge-\\frac1{2H}\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nA_i\\ge-8K.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\operatorname{val}(i)=0\\), the approximation places \\(A_i/S\\) at least \\(-1/(8H)\\), so the index is included. If \\(\\operatorname{val}(i)&gt;0\\), the same conclusion holds. If \\(\\operatorname{val}(i)&lt;0\\), the value gap in Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{A_i}{S}\n\\le-\\frac1H+\\frac1{8H}\n=-\\frac7{8H},\n\\]\nso the index is excluded. This proves exact correctness, including equality at zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(F,x,N,K,S,J,Y,Z\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vectors \\(Y,Z\\) satisfy\n\\[\nF(Y)\\ge Y,\\qquad\nF(Z)\\le Z,\\qquad\nY\\le x\\le Z,\n\\qquad\n\\|Y\\|_\\infty,\\|Z\\|_\\infty\\le J.\n\\]\nFor every coordinate \\(i\\),\n\\[\nZ_i-Y_i\\le2(N+1)=K/128.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The inequalities\n\\[\nx-(N+1)\\mathbf1\\le Y\\le x-N\\mathbf1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nx+N\\mathbf1\\le Z\\le x+(N+1)\\mathbf1,\n\\]\ntogether with\n\\[\n\\gamma(N+1)=N-\\frac1N\\le N,\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\nF(Y)\\ge x-\\gamma(N+1)\\mathbf1\\ge x-N\\mathbf1\\ge Y\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF(Z)\\le x+\\gamma(N+1)\\mathbf1\\le x+N\\mathbf1\\le Z.\n\\]\nHere monotonicity and the translation identity for \\(F\\) were used. The remaining conclusions follow from these defining bounds and\n\\[\n\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le SW.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the outer box procedure and all constants of Proposition 3. Each call to \\(\\mathcal L\\) uses uniform masses \\(a_i=b_i=1/n\\), and satisfies Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every box in the iteration contains \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\). The iteration makes at most\n\\[\n128(d_0+1)\n\\]\ncalls to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and ends with \\(D\\le K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 and \\(D_0\\ge2J\\) establish initial containment.</p><p>Suppose the current box contains \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\). Put\n\\[\nu=A+D\\mathbf1,\\qquad P=D/32.\n\\]\nWithin the current box, \\(Y\\) is a subsolution and \\(Z\\) is a supersolution, and their support masses are at most one. Consequently, Proposition 2 has the following contrapositive form:\n\\[\n\\ell_i=+\\Longrightarrow Z_i&gt;A_i+P,\n\\qquad\n\\ell_i=-\\Longrightarrow Y_i&lt;u_i-P.\n\\]\nFor example, \\(Z_i\\le A_i+P\\) would be a minus claim and would require a minus label.</p><p>In the case \\(h=128\\), the formula for \\(D'\\) has \\(d\\ge1\\), because \\(D&gt;K&gt;255\\). If \\(h&gt;128\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta}{D}=\\frac1h\\le\\frac1{128};\n\\]\nif \\(h=128\\), this ratio is \\(1/256\\). Thus\n\\[\n0&lt;\\delta\\le D/128,\n\\qquad\nZ_i-Y_i\\le K/128&lt;D/128.\n\\]</p><p>At a plus coordinate, these inequalities give\n\\[\nY_i\n&gt;Z_i-D/128\n&gt;A_i+D/32-D/128\n=A_i+3D/128\n\\ge A_i+\\delta.\n\\]\nThe lower endpoint can therefore increase by \\(\\delta\\); the upper endpoint remains unchanged.</p><p>At a minus coordinate,\n\\[\nZ_i\n&lt;Y_i+D/128\n&lt;u_i-D/32+D/128\n=u_i-3D/128\n\\le u_i-\\delta.\n\\]\nThe upper endpoint can decrease by \\(\\delta\\); the lower endpoint remains unchanged. This proves the induction step for both comparison vectors.</p><p>There are only \\(128\\) permitted mantissas at any exponent, and the exponent never increases. The strictly decreasing sequence of widths therefore has at most \\(128(d_0+1)\\) terms before reaching the stopping range, which contains all permitted widths at exponent zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Section 4.3, bit complexity and uniformity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the explicitly encoded game of Theorem 1, with complete binary input length \\(L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the constants, scaled map, and outer algorithm of Proposition 3, implementing its labeling calls by the procedure of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Integer and rational arithmetic is exact. Positive rational masses may be stored as unreduced fractions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algorithm is implementable by a uniform deterministic Turing machine and uses\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations, including input reading, all exact arithmetic, and output.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 gives polynomial bit cost for initialization and for evaluations of \\(\\lfloor F\\rfloor\\). Lemma 4.2 bounds the number of recursion nodes in one outer call.</p><p>All masses can be stored as unreduced positive fractions. An edge that changes masses multiplies each by one of a fixed set of rational constants, and so increases its numerator and denominator lengths by at most a constant. The other edge leaves masses unchanged. Since a path contains at most \\(B_0=O(\\log(n+1))\\) mass-changing edges, the mass lengths are polynomial, indeed \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\). Every comparison, update, and support-independent base test has polynomial bit cost. The algorithm does not compute \\(B\\); it is used only for the analysis.</p><p>Outside its children, a nonbase call performs \\(O(n+1)\\) pivot updates and associated vector operations, all of polynomial bit cost. In a width base case,\n\\[\nd&lt;12\\Longrightarrow D\\le255\\cdot2^{11},\n\\]\nan absolute constant, so the clipped-floor iteration uses at most \\(1+nD\\) polynomial-cost evaluations. The mass base case is also polynomial. Consequently, the node bound of Lemma 4.2 bounds the bit cost of one outer call up to a polynomial factor. Lemma 3.2 bounds the number of outer calls by a polynomial as well.</p><p>Finally, all loops, tie rules, integer operations, and recursive calls are specified by finite algorithms independent of the input. A depth-first implementation stores a stack of polynomial depth whose individual records have polynomial length. Scanning such a stack or copying a record on a deterministic Turing machine costs at most a polynomial factor per local operation. The final integer tests \\(A_i\\ge-8K\\) and the output take polynomial time. Absorbing these factors into the recursion-tree bound proves the stated uniform deterministic bit complexity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Section 4.3, polynomial-length data and exact map evaluation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the game, constants, boxes, and scaled map of Proposition 4. At a chance vertex, write each encoded probability as\n\\[\np(e)=c_e/d_e,\\qquad d_e&gt;0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The integers \\(W,Q,A_0,H,N,K,S,J,D_0\\) have polynomial binary length, and\n\\[\n\\log_2Q=O(L+1),\\qquad\n\\log_2W=O(L+1),\\qquad\n\\log_2A_0=O((L+2)^2),\n\\]\n\\[\nd_0=O((L+2)^2).\n\\]\nAll recursive box endpoints, pivots, and integer arguments to \\(F\\) have polynomial binary length. An evaluation of \\(F(t)\\) at any such integer vector \\(t\\), and its coordinatewise floor, has polynomial bit cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since every vertex has an outgoing edge and the input is explicit,\n\\[\nn,|E|=O(L+1).\n\\]\nThe binary lengths of the probabilities and rewards give\n\\[\n\\log_2Q=O(L+1),\\qquad\n\\log_2W=O(L+1),\\qquad\n\\log_2A_0=O((L+2)^2).\n\\]\nIn particular, all integers defining the scaled map and initial box have \\(O((L+2)^2)\\) bits, and \\(d_0=O((L+2)^2)\\). Reading the input and forming these integers by ordinary exact arithmetic take polynomial time.</p><p>Every recursive box lies in its parent by Lemma 2.2, and every outer box lies in the initial box. Thus all box endpoints, pivot coordinates, and integer arguments to \\(F\\) have polynomial binary length.</p><p>For an integer vector \\(t\\), the quantities used to evaluate \\(F_i(t)\\) are\n\\[\n\\frac{Sw(e)+(N-1)t_j}{N},\n\\qquad e:i\\to j.\n\\]\nMax and Min take exact extrema. At a chance vertex, the weighted sum can be formed with common denominator \\(QN\\) and integer numerator\n\\[\n\\sum_{e:i\\to j}\nc_e\\frac Q{d_e}\\bigl(Sw(e)+(N-1)t_j\\bigr).\n\\]\nEvery \\(d_e\\) divides \\(Q\\), even when input fractions are not reduced. This formula gives polynomial bit lengths and polynomial bit cost for an evaluation and its coordinatewise floors. Zero probabilities and parallel edges require no special handling.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Section 4.3, recursion-tree bound", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider one outer call to \\(\\mathcal L\\), initially with \\(a_i=b_i=1/n\\), whose width exponent is at most \\(d_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a recursive call define\n  \\[\n  \\mu=\\min_i a_ib_i,\\qquad\n  \\rho=\\frac{32}{25},\\qquad\n  B(\\mu)=\\min\\{j\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}:\\rho^j\\mu&gt;1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B_0=B(1/n^2),\\qquad\n  \\Lambda=d_0+B_0,\\qquad\n  s_*=2048n+3.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has \\(B_0=O(\\log(n+1))\\), including \\(B_0=1\\) when \\(n=1\\). The recursion depth is at most \\(\\Lambda\\), and any root-to-node path has at most \\(B_0\\) edges decreasing \\(B\\).</p><p>The number of nodes is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{\\Lambda}\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min(\\ell,B_0)}\n\\binom{\\ell}{j}s_*^j\n\\le\n(\\Lambda+1)(B_0+1)(1+\\Lambda s_*)^{B_0}\n=\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The initial value \\(B_0=B(1/n^2)\\) is \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\); this includes \\(n=1\\), for which \\(B_0=1\\).</p><p>By Lemma 2.3, every recursive edge decreases \\(d+B\\). Only the provisional-label child can preserve \\(B\\), and it decreases \\(d\\) by one. Each other child decreases \\(B\\) by at least one and does not increase \\(d\\). Thus the depth is at most\n\\[\n\\Lambda=d_0+B_0,\n\\]\nand any root-to-node path contains at most \\(B_0\\) edges decreasing \\(B\\).</p><p>At each node there is at most one \\(B\\)-preserving child and at most\n\\[\ns_*=2(1024n+1)+1=2048n+3\n\\]\nother children: the two pivot passes account for the first term and the reweighted call for the final one.</p><p>A path of length \\(\\ell\\) with \\(j\\) edges decreasing \\(B\\) is specified by their \\(j\\) positions and at most \\(s_*\\) choices at each such position; the other child, if present, is unique. Hence the total number of nodes is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{\\Lambda}\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min(\\ell,B_0)}\n\\binom{\\ell}{j}s_*^j\n\\le\n(\\Lambda+1)(B_0+1)(1+\\Lambda s_*)^{B_0}.\n\\]\nUsing \\(d_0=O((L+2)^2)\\), \\(n=O(L+1)\\), and \\(B_0=O(\\log(n+1))\\), this is\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}.\n\\]\nThe depth may be polynomial in \\(L\\), but branching choices occur only \\(O(\\log(n+1))\\) times on any path.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 13, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 16, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 17, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [9, 18, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 6, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 0, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 16, "cite"]]});
